          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line drug therapy for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "The guidance I found does not name one drug as first-line for everyone. It describes a general approach instead.\n\nAccording to the National diabetes association standards of care summary (guideline \"Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management\"), metformin, used together with structured lifestyle changes, is the usual starting drug when there is no other major condition that changes the choice. The same summary says metformin has long experience behind it, is low cost, is weight neutral, and carries little risk of low blood sugar on its own. Kidney function is checked before starting and from time to time afterward.\n\nThe same document says current guidance is different from older advice. The first choice now depends on other conditions a person has, and for some of them the choice is made without looking only at the HbA1c blood test result.\n\nThe search did not return the details of those other conditions or which drugs apply to them, so I can't tell you more about those cases. A clinician can explain what fits a particular situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
